2186045417] [17592186479767 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479767 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479767 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4670dc2b-4eab-5b4e-ad02-4eea217ed995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0d9b917-d0b0-5b1f-9a90-f69dcef445f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161848" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The function $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nII. The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)={f}^{′}(a).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bbe6736-f555-565a-b497-ef9dcdc55838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479832 #edb/ref 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52539dbe-37de-5500-97cf-839689e21baa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479853 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1-(-2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (x+y)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/53/539115fd470666cb84e3de4b9b3471db6f04d0af6fd3e33897e5115361fe4e7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=1.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\nII. ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=3$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58964444-2a46-54cf-b2ee-de1f5f5033d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479856 #edb/ref 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-3$ since ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $y=-0.87=f(-1),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=-1,$ for example, $(-2,0),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=-1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(-1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=3=f(1),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
